ant industry in ghana but  all of the bigger factories have since  closed down one of the principal reasons  was mismanagement audrius darko wanted  to find out more about it she  specialized in agricultural sciences and  decided to go talk to farmers directly  i realized that the culture was  different  they had been used to conventional  agriculture making use of a lot of  chemicals and how they planted how they  prepare their land burning crop residue  in the fields is also a common practice  despite people here feeling the effects  of climate change at first hand there's  been a change in the amount of rainfall  and harvests are diminishing the fires  put a big strain on the already severely  depleted soil  nature it's supposed to work with us  to better our lives but seeing us humans  degrading it on a daily by burning it by  destroying the biodiversity  and engaging in bad practices  that put put um  that put the fauna on the floor and in  extinction is is just frustrating for me  because we should be appreciative of  what nature gives us this community is  located directly on the avu lagoon an  important wetland  the canals are used to transport the  harvest and growing sugarcane itself  requires a lot of water  it's an area rich in biodiversity but  the use of chemicals in farming is  damaging the environment and there are  fewer and fewer fish  philip tomato and his father are  noticing that too  they own around five hectares of land  and have a big family to support  all the more reason for them to rethink  things  my fear is that if we continue applying  chemicals on our farmlands our children  will suffer  and they'll find it difficult to  cultivate sugarcane in the future  audrey starco occasionally takes a break  from the countryside for a trip to akra  in ghana cities urban farming is a new  trend hastened by the pandemic  audrey knows paulina from her university  days the software developer is one of  150 or so city dwellers who have their  organic fertilizer delivered to their  door  i plugged some seeds  and prepared across to you with that and  it was like amazing i plugged about  about 15 or so of them and professed you  with that end  i had that feeling that okay i am eating  something i planted like it was that  good  the countryside might be a world away  from the city but audrey starco enjoys  her new rural life  she's the only member of her team to  work full-time for the project which she  founded in 2018 together with other  students from the university of accra  they also developed the fertilizer in  the university lab  she wants many more farmers to become  aware of its benefits  it would transform their mindsets  about seeing waste as a resource  and giving them more knowledge and more  urgent zeal  to pass past this information to their  children and their grandchildren  andreas darko knows how to get her point  across and has gained the respect of the  farmers  now she just needs to find financial  backers in order for the savon sake  project to flourish in its full  potential  you
 

